--- A stray word after an `if`/`while` condition is refused with a message that
--- misleads: the block DOES have an indented body. Written by a Perl/Fortran/
--- shell reflex (`ge` for `gte`, `le` for `lte`); any word does it (`k foo 0`).
---
--- Interpreted (crow run -i) and compiled (crow run), the same refusal:
---   imaginary_base_numbers-1.co:17:10: the block opened on line 17 has no indented body — indent its lines under it
---     17 │     if k ge 0
---        │          ^^
--- Expected: words that name the real problem, as the other forms already do:
---   `x = k ge 0` says "a line holds one statement — 'ge' starts a second one:
---   put it on a line of its own", and `if k >= 0` says "Crow uses the word
---   'gte', not '>=': a gte b." `ge`, `le` (and `geq`, `leq`?) would deserve the
---   teaching error `>=` gets; at the least the condition's stray word should be
---   named, not the (present) body called missing. Same for `while k ge 0`.

fn f[k]
    if k ge 0
        return 1
    return 0

log[f[2]]
